2.5 Heterozygosity Testing

loci |&gt;
  is_heterozygote()

[1] FALSE  TRUE  TRUE FALSE FALSE


Within a data frame, the same test summarizes observed heterozygosity by stratum:

data( arapat )

arapat |&gt;
  mutate( het = is_heterozygote( LTRS ) ) |&gt;
  group_by( Species ) |&gt;
  summarize( Ho_LTRS = mean( het, na.rm = TRUE ) )

# A tibble: 3 × 2
  Species   Ho_LTRS
  &lt;fct&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Cape        0.107
2 Mainland    0.472
3 Peninsula   0.242

5.5 Individual Heterozygosity
The Hi mode is estimated per individual rather than per locus. It returns one row for each individual, keeping the non-locus columns, so it can be summarized by any grouping you like:

arapat |&gt;
  genetic_diversity( mode = "Hi" ) |&gt;
  group_by( Species ) |&gt;
  summarize( mean_Hi = mean( Hi ) )

# A tibble: 3 × 2
  Species   mean_Hi
  &lt;fct&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Cape        0.117
2 Mainland    0.301
3 Peninsula   0.275

9.8 Default behaviour on a real graph
Nothing above is specific to simulated data. For an existing Population Graph the defaults give a complete picture in one line, here for lopho, the Population Graph of the Sonoran Desert cactus Lophocereus.

data( lopho )

lopho |&gt;
  gravity_field() |&gt;
  plot()

10.3 Migration Events
A migration_event pairs a migration matrix with a generation interval, allowing temporal regime changes:

# Low migration for generations 1-50
evt1 &lt;- c( "A", "B", "C" ) |&gt;
  migration_matrix( model = "island", m = 0.05 ) |&gt;
  migration_event( start = 1, end = 50 )

# High migration from generation 51 onward
evt2 &lt;- c( "A", "B", "C" ) |&gt;
  migration_matrix( model = "island", m = 0.20 ) |&gt;
  migration_event( start = 51 )

11.5 Single-Parent Exclusion
The parent_finder() function identifies compatible parents for each offspring using exclusion. It requires a data.frame with ID and OffID columns, where adults have OffID = 0:

# Prepare a combined data.frame: adults (OffID = 0) then offspring
locus_cols &lt;- adults |&gt;
  column_class( "locus" )

combined &lt;- bind_rows( adults |&gt;
                        select( ID, all_of( locus_cols ) ) |&gt;
                        mutate( OffID = 0 ),
                       offs |&gt;
                        select( ID, all_of( locus_cols ) ) |&gt;
                        mutate( OffID = row_number() ) )

combined |&gt;
  parent_finder() |&gt;
  head()

  ID OffID ParentID      T
2  1     1        2 0.1250
1  1     1        1 0.0625
3  1     1        5 0.0625
4  1     1       10 0.0625
5  1     1       19 0.0625
6  1     2        1 0.0625


Its T column is the one-parent compatibility score from transition_probability() (without freqs): it ranks the compatible parents of each offspring, but it is not a likelihood.

12.4 Interactive Leaflet Maps
addAlleleFrequencies() adds allele-frequency pie charts to a leaflet map and follows the standard leaflet pipe pattern. Pre-compute frequencies with frequencies(), build the base map from strata_coordinates(), then pipe into addAlleleFrequencies().

library( leaflet )
library( leaflet.minicharts )

freqs &lt;- arapat |&gt;
  frequencies( loci = "LTRS", stratum = "Species" )

arapat |&gt;
  strata_coordinates( stratum = "Species" ) |&gt;
  leaflet() |&gt;
  addTiles() |&gt;
  addAlleleFrequencies( freqs )





Pass locus = explicitly when freqs contains more than one locus. Width and height of the pie charts (in pixels) are controlled with width = and height =.
